# NELA risk model in WebAssembly

`risk-score-service/src/risk-calculator.R` is supplied to us and is not
edited; it stays the specification of the model. R cannot be compiled to
WebAssembly (it is interpreted; the only way to run it in a browser is to
ship the R interpreter and its package filesystem), so this package keeps
a **compiled translation** of that file - the same coefficients, the same
winsorising, the same category mapping, R's rounding - in Rust, built to
a 7.6 kB WebAssembly module with no runtime around it. What makes the
translation safe is the parity test: every row the service was checked
with is scored by both, and the build fails on any difference.

## Proof

`bun run test` runs the Rust unit tests, then `parity.test.ts`, which
scores every row of the reference sheet and both ASC Audit extracts and
compares with the service's scores held in them, plus a fresh sample
against the **live** service:

| Sheet | Rows compared | Differ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reference sheet `sim_data_v4` (pre-op) | 4,940 | 0 |
| ASC Audit extract, pre-op | 3,352 | 0 |
| ASC Audit extract, post-op | 3,266 | 0 |

Rows the service refuses (an input missing) are the rows with no expected
score, and the module gives none there either. Rounding is R's
(`round(x, 1)`, halves to even); `exp` and `log` are `libm`, so every
browser gives the same number regardless of its own `Math`.

When the supplied R changes, `src/lib.rs` must change with it, and this
test is what says so.

## Build

    bun run build

runs `cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` (Rust with
the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target) and writes `dist/nela_risk_wasm.wasm`
and `dist/risk-wasm.bundle.js` - the plain-script wrapper with the module
embedded as base64, which loads itself on arrival.

## In the ASC Audit pages

Upload `dist/risk-wasm.bundle.js` to the database as a file resource and
include it on each form before the page script:

```html
<script src="/RCOA/NELAUAT2.nsf/risk-wasm.bundle.js"></script>
```

The four pages (`docs/nela/old/EmLap-v12.js`, `EmLap-v13.js`, `NoLap-v3.js`,
`NoLap-v2.js`) have

```js
var RISK = { engine: "wasm" }   // or "service"
```

and their computed score fields call `computeRisk(kind, payload, signal)`:
`riskWasm.compute(kind, payload)` when the engine is `"wasm"` and the module
has loaded, `requestRisk` (the service) otherwise and as the fallback. The
computed field runs it in the background either way: the page never
waits, a newer set of inputs abandons an older computation, and the value
is posted with the record and shared with a collaboration room like any
other. `RISK.engine = "service"` goes back to the request.

`payload` is what the page already posts to the service (`S01AgeOnArrival`,
`S03ASAScore`, …, the `S03Ind_*` indications as 0/1, and the malignancy /
soiling / urgency fields of the score wanted). Values may be numbers or
strings.

## Files

| File | What |
| --- | --- |
| `src/lib.rs` | the translation of `risk-calculator.R`, with its own tests |
| `risk-wasm.js` | the page wrapper: `riskWasm.load()`, `.score()`, `.compute()` |
| `build.ts` | cargo build + the bundle |
| `parity.test.ts` | the proof above |
| `dist/` | build output (ignored) |
